--- a/robot-docs/migrations.md
+++ b/robot-docs/migrations.md
@@ -234,3 +234,40 @@ checkpoint belong to the fork. Forks copy no admission receipts or live runs.
 Acceptance guarantees stored input or its committed or cancelled outcome.
 Commit means the input reached the transcript. Neither guarantee promises that
 assistant generation, tools, or external effects occur exactly once.
+
+## persisted session capabilities and lifecycle transactions
+
+`daemon/session_requirements` owns the core `session_requirements` table.
+`conversation.initialise` initializes it beside session configuration, independent
+of extension installation. Each `(session, capability)` row records a required
+complete active request-context owner. Missing or unreadable storage is an error;
+removing the extension cannot turn a required session into an ordinary session.
+Initialization atomically records `session_requirements_v1` in core migrations.
+An unmarked stock database receives the table; a marked database with missing
+or incompatible storage refuses startup rather than recreating an empty table.
+Binding owners call `require_in` or `release_in` on their transaction connection
+so binding changes and host requirements commit or roll back together. Native
+session deletion removes requirement rows; it does not delete external identities.
+
+`harness/session_lifecycle` defines trusted connection-only creation callbacks.
+Runtime captures canonical lifecycle owners once during installation, including
+quarantined declarations and owners disabled for individual sessions. Guards
+perform bounded reads; mutation callbacks use only the supplied SQLite connection.
+They must not reenter actors, nest transactions, notify the bus, start processes,
+or call external services. Errors and catchable crashes roll back the entire
+native transaction. A required quarantined owner reports its canonical installation
+failure, including when a duplicate declaration was rejected first.
+
+`conversation` calls lifecycle hooks inside native and identified creation
+transactions. Identified receipt recovery does not rerun callbacks. `history`
+provides exact ordered source/branch references for copied fork rows, excluding
+synthetic tool completions, and preserves native LCM prefix inheritance. Initial
+child tasks commit with their session and family membership; registry notification
+and activation follow commit.
+
+Inheritance of persisted capabilities is not supported in this slice. Core
+child, fork, and rewind transactions reject a source with any persisted requirement
+before writes, even if the contributing extension is absent. The session actor
+also rejects rewind summary preparation before opening a kernel or provider call.
+Authenticated native service binding mutations use the session's idle gate;
+ordinary model commands cannot invoke that mutation bridge.
